'''Lombardic''' or '''Langobardic''' ({{langx|de|Langobardisch}}) is an extinct [[West Germanic language]] that was spoken by the [[Lombards]] ({{lang|la|Langobardi}}), the [[Germanic people]] who settled in present-day [[Italy]] in the sixth century and established the [[Kingdom of the Lombards]]. It was already declining by the seventh century because the invaders quickly adopted the [[Vulgar Latin]] spoken by the local population. Many toponyms in [[Lombardy|modern Lombardy]] and [[Lombardy (historical region)|Greater Lombardy]] ([[Northern Italy]]) and items of the [[Lombard language]] and broader [[Gallo-Italic languages|Gallo-Italic]] vocabulary derive from Lombardic.

Lombardic is a {{lang|de|[[Corpus language|Trümmersprache]]}} (literally, 'rubble-language'), that is, a language preserved only in fragmentary form: there are no texts in Lombardic, only individual words and personal names cited in [[Latin]] [[code of law|law codes]], histories and [[charter]]s. As a result, there are many aspects of the language about which nothing is known.{{sfn|Tischler|1989|p=195}}{{sfn|Francovich Onesti| 2014|p=1}}

Some scholars have proposed that the modern [[Cimbrian language|Cimbrian]] and [[Mòcheno language|Mòcheno]] languages are descended from Lombardic, but this is rejected by a majority of scholars.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Services |first=Diplomatic Language |date=2019-05-02 |title=The Cimbrian Language |url=https://dlsdc.com/blog/the-cimbrian-language/ |access-date=2024-08-11 |website=Diplomatic Language Services |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Classification==
Lombardic is classified as part of the [[Upper German]] group of [[West Germanic]] languages, descended from [[Elbe Germanic]], and most closely related to its geographical neighbours [[Alemannic German|Alemannic]] and [[Bavarian language|Bavarian]]. This is consistent with the accounts of classical historians, and indeed with the archaeological evidence of Langobardic settlement along the river [[Elbe]].{{sfn|Christie|1995|p=5}}

In view of the lack of Lombardic texts and the narrow scope of the attested Lombardic vocabulary—almost entirely nouns in the nominative case and proper names—the classification rests entirely on [[phonology]]. Here the clear evidence of the [[High German consonant shift|Second Sound Shift]] shows that the language must be [[High German]], rather than [[North Sea Germanic]] or [[East Germanic]], as some earlier scholars proposed.{{sfn|Maurer|1952|pp=51–52}}

==The Lombardic Corpus==
The main evidence for Lombardic comes from contemporary documents written in Latin, where (a) individual Lombardic terms are cited and (b) people with Lombardic names are mentioned. There are also a small number of inscriptions, a handful of which use the [[runes|runic alphabet]]. Additional information about the vocabulary of Lombardic comes from later-attested [[loan words]] into [[Italian language|Italian]] and its dialects, as well as a large number of Italian [[toponym|place names]] of Lombardic origin (see [[#Influence on Italian and Lombard|below]]).

The documentary sources fall into three categories:{{sfn|Francovich Onesti|2014|p=3}}
# [[Lombard law|Lombardic law codes]]
# [[Narrative history|Narrative histories]]
# Administrative documents of the [[Kingdom of the Lombards|Lombard kingdom]] such as [[charters]].

==Phonology==
Establishing sound values for Lombardic is problematic for two reasons. Where words are attested in contemporary Lombardic documents, scribes trained in Latin could not be expected to record accurately, or even consistently, the sounds of Lombardic. In the case of loanwords, these are often attested much later, by which time their form will have been affected not only by the adaptation to the phonology of the various [[Gallo-Italian|Gallo-Italic languages]] but also by subsequent sound changes in the development of [[Italian language|Italian]].{{sfn|Bruckner |1895 |p=37}}{{sfn|Falloumini|2015 |p=111}}

===Vowels===
The vowel system of Lombardic is very conservative and largely preserves the [[Proto-Germanic language|Proto-Germanic]] system.{{sfn|Mitzka|1951|p=4}} The three main vowel developments characteristic of other Upper German dialects are lacking in Lombardic.
*There is no evidence of the [[Germanic umlaut|Primary Umlaut]] of /{{IPA|a}}/, which is prevalent in [[Old High German]] (OHG), e.g. Lombardic {{lang|lng|camphio}} = OHG {{lang|goh|chemphio}} ("champion").{{sfn|Bruckner|1895|p=57}}
* The [[diphthong]] /{{IPA|ai}}/ is preserved where it becomes /{{IPA|ei}}/ in other Old High German dialects, but similarly to all other dialects it becomes [[monophthong]]ized to /{{IPA|a:}}/ or /{{IPA|e:}}/ before /{{IPA|r}}/ and to /{{IPA|e:}}/ before /{{IPA|w}}/ in the 8th century. Examples: Lgb. {{lang|lng|schuldhais}} = OHG {{lang|goh|scultheizo}} ("mayor");{{sfn|Bruckner|1895|p=100}} Lgb. {{lang|lng|rairaub}}  = OHG {{lang|goh|rêroup}} ("body snatching")
* The diphthong /{{IPA|au}}/ is preserved, whereas in other Old High German dialects it becomes /{{IPA|ou}}/ or is [[monophthong]]ized to /{{IPA|o:}}/ in certain phonetic contexts. Example: Lgb. {{lang|lng|launegild}} = OHG {{lang|goh|lôngelt}} ("payment").{{sfn|Bruckner|1895|p=105}}
* The mid long vowels /{{IPA|e:}}/ and /{{IPA|o:}}/, which are diphthongized in OHG to /{{IPA|ea~ia}}/ and /{{IPA|uo}}/ respectively, remain unchanged in Lombardic. Examples: Lgb. {{lang|lng|mêta}} = OHG {{lang|goh|miata}} ("price");{{sfn|Bruckner|1895|p=90}} Lgb. {{lang|lng|plôvum}} = OHG {{lang|goh|phluog}} ("plough").{{sfn|Bruckner|1895|p=93}}

===Consonants===
Lombardic participated in and indeed shows some of the earliest evidence for the [[High German consonant shift]]. The ''[[Historia Langobardorum]]'' of [[Paulus Diaconus]] mentions a duke [[Zaban]] of 574, showing {{IPA|/t/}} shifted to {{IPA|/ts/}}. The term {{lang|lng|stolesazo}} (ablative)<ref>''Edictus rothari'', cap. 150: "[...] districtus ab stolesazo."</ref> (German: ''[[:de:Stuhlsasse|Stuhlsasse]]''; the second element is cognate with English ''seat'') in the ''[[Edictum Rothari]]'' shows the same shift. Many names in the Lombard royal families show shifted consonants, particularly {{IPA|/b/}} > {{IPA|/p/}} in the following name components:
* ''-bert'' > ''-pert'': Aripert, Godepert
* ''-berg'' > ''-perg'': Gundperga (daughter of [[Agilulf|King Agilulf]])
* ''-brand'' > ''-prand'': Ansprand, Liutprand

This [[sound change]] left two different sets of nouns in the [[Italian language]]: {{lang|it|palco}} (< Lombardic {{lang|lng|palk}}, "beam") vs. {{lang|it|balcone}} (< Lombardic {{lang|lng|balk}}, "wood platform"); {{lang|it|panca}} (< Lombardic {{lang|lng|panka}}) vs. {{lang|it|banca}} (Lombardic {{lang|lng|banka}}, "bench").<ref>Giacomo Devoto: ''Dizionario etimologico''.</ref>

==Decline==
It is not possible to say with certainty when the Lombardic language died out and there are divergent views on the issue.{{sfn|Francovich Onesti|2014|p=9}} It seems certain that it was in decline even before the end of the Lombardic kingdom in 774, though it may have survived longer in Northern areas, with their denser Lombardic settlement.{{sfn|Meyer| 1877| p=5}}{{sfn|Gamillscheg |2017|p=200}} In any case, the Lombard host which had invaded Italy was not monolingual: in addition to a sizeable body of [[Saxons]], there were also "[[Gepids]], [[Bulgars]], [[Sarmatians]], [[Pannonians]], [[Suevi]], [[Noricum|Noricans]] and so on" (''Historia Langobardorum'', II, 26).{{sfn|Christie|1995|p=64}}

In the areas of Italy settled by the Lombards, "there followed a rapid mixing of Roman and barbarian, especially among the population settled on the land."{{sfn|Todd|2004|p=246}} The Lombard conversion from [[Arianism]] to [[Roman Catholicism]] in the 7th century would have removed a major barrier to the integration of the two populations.{{sfn|Hutterer|1999|pp=337–338}}{{sfn|Francovich Onesti|2002|p=11}} By the 8th century speakers of Lombardic were bilingual, adopting the local [[Gallo-Italic languages|Gallo-Italic]] language.{{sfn|Tischler|1989|p=195}}

Even as use of the language declined, Lombardic personal names remained popular, though they gradually   lost their connection to the source language, adopting Latin endings. The 8th century also saw the development of hybrid names with both Lombardic and Latin elements (e.g. ''Alipertulus'' = Lgb {{lang|lng|Alipert}} + Lat. {{lang|la|-ulus}}).{{sfn|Francovich Onesti|2002|p=2}} By this time occurrence of both Lombardic and Latin names within a single family "is so widespread that such cases make up the majority throughout Lombard Italy".{{sfn|Francovich Onesti|2002|p=17}}

Explicit evidence of the death of Lombardic comes in the late 10th century: the [[Chronicon Salernitanum|Salerno Chronicle]] mentions the "German language which the Lombards previously spoke" ({{lang|la|lingua todesca, quod olim Langobardi loquebantur}}, cap. 38). But some knowledge of Lombardic remained: the Salerno chronicler nonetheless knows that the Lombardic term {{lang|lng|stoleseyz}} includes an element which means "sitting" ({{lang|la|sedendo}}).{{sfn|Bruckner|1895|p=14}} As late as 1003, a charter uses the Lombardic term {{lang|lng|scarnafol}} ("filthy fellow") as an insult.{{sfn|Naumann|Betz|1962 |p=22}}

==Influence on Italian and Lombard==
===Loan words===

At least 280 Italian words have been identified as Lombardic [[loanword|loans]], though there is wide local variation and some are found only in areas settled by the Lombards.{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|pp=174-5}} One problem in detecting Lombardic loans is that they are not always readily distinguishable from [[Gothic language|Gothic]], the language of the previous Germanic rulers of Italy. In many cases, it is only evidence of the Second Sound Shift, which did not affect Gothic, that guarantees a Lombardic source for a loanword.{{sfn|Rohlfs|1947|p=9}} However, the Sound Shift is equally present in Alemannic and Bavarian, which are also potential sources of loans into Northern Italian varieties at this period.{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=128}}

The main areas of the Lombardic vocabulary surviving in Italian are: warfare and weapons, the law, government and society, housebuilding and the household, objects and activities from daily life. Of these, however, Lombardic government and legal terms were to a great extent superseded by the [[Gallo-Romance languages|Gallo-Roman]] vocabulary of the [[Kingdom of Italy (Holy Roman Empire)|Frankish conquest]].{{sfn|Vennemann|2003|pp=13-14}}{{sfn|Francovich Onesti|2014|p=3}} The predominance of loans relating to daily life "would appear to be a sign that the Longobards fitted in and integrated with the locals at a grass-roots level."{{sfn|Christie| 1995|p=167}}

'''Examples''':{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|pp=129–174|ps=, with many other examples.}}
* {{lang|it|anca}}, "hip" < lgb. {{lang|lng|hanka}}
* {{lang|it|balcone}}, "balcony", and {{lang|it|palco}}, "shelf" < lgb. {{lang|lng|balk}}
* {{lang|it|bussare}}, "knock" < lgb {{lang|lng|bauʒʒan}}
* {{lang|it|faida}}, "blood feud" <lgb. {{lang|lng|faihida}}
* {{lang|it|graffa}}, "brace" < lgb. {{lang|lng|krāpfo}} "hook"
* {{lang|it|guancia}} "cheek", < lgb {{lang|lng|wangja}}
* {{lang|it|gufo}}, "owl" < lgb. {{lang|lng|gôfjan}} "cry out"
* {{lang|it|lesto}}, "fast" < lgb. {{lang|lng|list}} "cleverness"
* {{lang|it|melma}}, "mud" < lgb. {{lang|lng|melm}}
* {{lang|it|nocca}}, "knuckle" < lgb. {{lang|lng|knohha}}
* {{lang|it|panca}}, "bench" < lgb. {{lang|lng|banka}}, {{lang|lng|panka}}
* {{lang|it|russare}}, "snore" < lgb. {{lang|lng|hrûʒʒan}}
* {{lang|it|scaglia}}, "scale, skin" < lgb. {{lang|lng|skalja}}
* {{lang|it|taccola}}, "jackdaw" < lgb {{lang|lng|tâhhala}} 
* {{lang|it|zazzera}}, "mop of hair" < lgb. {{lang|lng|zazza}}.

The [[Lombard language]] is a distinct [[Romance languages|Romance language]] spoken in Northern Italy and [[Switzerland]]. It, too, has loans from Lombardic. The following examples come from [[Bergamasque dialect|Bergamasque]], an [[Eastern Lombard dialect]].{{sfn|Sizzi|2014}}{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|pp=134–157}}

* {{lang|lmo|blösen}}, "chopped hay" < lgb. {{lang|lng|blôsem}} ("flower")
* {{lang|lmo|breda}}, "cultivated field" < lgb. {{lang|lng|braida}} ("open plain")
* {{lang|lmo|garb}}, "sour, unripe" < lgb. {{lang|lng|harwi}}
* {{lang|lmo|margnöch}}, "stubborn" < lgb. {{lang|lng|mahr}} + {{lang|lng|knohha}} ("horse" + "bone/head")
* {{lang|lmo|ròsta}}, "railing" < lgb. {{lang|lng|hrausta}} ("bundle of branches")
* {{lang|lmo|scagna}}, it. {{lang|it|scranna}}, "chair" < lgb. {{lang|lng|skranna}} ("bench")
* {{lang|lmo|strobià:}}, "to clean the house" < lgb. {{lang|lng|straufinôn}} ("to rub away").

===Place names===

When the Lombards settled in Italy they had no previous acquaintance with Latin, with the result that the earliest Lombard settlements received Lombardic names.{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=62}} There are a number of distinct types of name.

Each [[Duke (Lombard)|Lombard duke]] was the lord of a group of military clans, who were settled in the area he ruled. The Lombardic term for such a clan was {{lang|lng|fara}}, and it has given its name (or the variant {{lang|lng|farra}}) to a number of Italian settlements, including:{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=62}}

*[[Fara Filiorum Petri]], Chieti, [[Abruzzo]]
*[[Fara Gera d'Adda]], Bergamo, [[Lombardy]]
*[[Fara San Martino]], Chieti, Abruzzo
*[[Fara in Sabina]], Rieti, [[Lazio]]
*[[Fara Novarese]], Novara, [[Piedmont]]
*[[Fara Olivana con Sola]],  Bergamo, Lombardy
*[[Fara Vicentino]], Vicenza, [[Veneto]]
*[[Farra d'Alpago]], Belluno, Veneto
*[[Farra di Soligo]], Treviso, Veneto
*[[Farra d'Isonzo]], Gorizia, [[Friuli-Venezia Giulia]]

Many settlements took their names from Lombardic personal names. For example the Lombardic name {{lang|lng|Gairo}} ("spear") is the source of: Noci Garrioni ([[Cremona]]), Garin ([[Turin]]), Garini ([[Province of Cuneo|Cuneo]] and [[Alessandria]]), Carengo ([[Novara]]), Ghiringhello ([[Verona]]), Gairilo ([[Brescia]]), Ghirla, (Verona), Garlasco ([[Pavia]]), Garleri (Porto Maurizio), and Garlazzolo (Pavia).{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=92}} Gamillscheg counts over 700 of these.{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=119}}

In many cases a Lombard personal name was appended to the Latin word for a natural feature.{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=108-119, with a count of 400.}} Thus Latin {{lang|la|collis}} ("hill") appears coupled with, for example, lgb. {{lang|lng|Alibert}} in Colle-Alberti ([[Florence]], [[Pisa]]), lgb. {{lang|lng|Gunzo}} in Collegonzi ([[Florence]]), and {{lang|lng|Raginwald}} in Collerinaldo ([[L'Aquila|Aquila]]).{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=112}}

Finally, there are over 30 Lombardic common nouns which have formed the basis for Italian place names, including:{{sfn|Gamillscheg|2017|p=63-69}}
*Lgb. {{lang|lng|berga}} ("mountain") > Berghi (Trient), Berga (Vicenza), Valperga (Turin)
*Lgb. {{lang|lng|skuldhaizo}} ("mayor") > Scaldasole (Pavia), Casale di Scodosia ([[Padua]])
*Lgb. {{lang|lng|stôdigard}} ("stud farm") > Stoerda (Novara) (cf. [[Stuttgart]]).

===Personal names===
A number of Lombardic personal names survive in modern Italy (for example, ''Aldo''), but where they have it is mostly in the form of a surname: ''[[Ansaldo (name)|Ansaldo]]'', ''[[Grimaldi (disambiguation)|Grimaldi]]'', ''[[Garibaldi (surname)|Garibaldi]]'', ''[[Landolfi]]'', ''[[Pandolfi]]'', ''Siccardi'' are all of Lombardic origin.{{sfn|Francovich Onesti|2014|p=2}}

==Sources==
===Latin===
There are a number of Latin texts that include Lombardic names, and Lombardic legal texts contain terms taken from the legal vocabulary of the vernacular, including:
* ''[[Origo gentis Langobardorum]]'' (7th century)
* [[Paulus Diaconus]], ''[[Historia Langobardorum]]'' {{circa|790 AD}}
* ''[[Historia Langobardorum codicis Gothani]]'' (9th century)
* ''[[Edictum Rothari]]'' (643 AD)

In 2006, Emilia Denčeva argued that the inscription of the [[Pernik sword]] may be Lombardic.{{sfn|Dentschewa|2006}}

===Runic===
There are two short inscriptions in the [[Elder Futhark]] which are regarded as Lombardic.{{sfn|Hutterer|1999|p=341}}

The [[Schretzheim]] bronze capsule, from 540–590:{{sfn|Siegmüller|2008a}}{{sfn|Naumann|Betz|1962|p=85}}{{sfn|Looijenga|2003|p=255}}
* On the lid: '''{{lang|lng|arogis d|italic=unset}}'''
* On the bottom: '''{{lang|lng|alaguþleuba dedun|italic=unset}}'''
:Translation: "Arogis and Alaguth (and) Leuba made (it)"{{sfn|Hutterer|1999|p=341}}
[[File:Breza futhark.jpg|thumb|The Futhark on the Breza half-column]]
The two [[Fibula (brooch)|fibulae]] from [[Bezenye]], [[Hungary]], from 510–590.{{sfn|Siegmüller|2008b}}{{sfn|Naumann|Betz|1962|p=86}}{{sfn|Looijenga|2003|p=230}}
* Fibula A: '''{{lang|lng|godahid unj[a]|italic=unset}}''' {{sfn|Siegmüller|2008b}}
* Fibula B: '''{{lang|lng|(k?)arsiboda segun|italic=unset}}''' {{sfn|Siegmüller|2008c}}
:Translation: "Godahi(l)d, (with) sympathy (I?) Arsiboda bless"{{sfn|Hutterer|1999|p=341}}

There is debate as to whether the inscription on the fifth-century [[Szabadbattyán]] [[belt buckle]]{{sfn|Siegmüller|2008d}} is Lombardic or [[Gothic language|Gothic]], and the reading is uncertain.{{sfn|Naumann|Betz|1962|p=85}}{{sfn|Looijenga|2003|p=148}}{{sfn|Krause|2014 |p=120}} The futhark on the Breza half-column is regarded as either Lombardic or [[Alemannic German|Alemannic]].{{sfn|Krause|2014 |p=285}}

